WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.928
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:10.115 --> 00:00:12.865
NuVu is an innovation school for middle and high school students.

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:19.280
Instead of having year-long classes, like math or English or something,

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:21.589
we have 2-week long studios.

00:00:21.589 --> 00:00:23.880
And so in these studios, these are projects where we're

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:25.790
presented with an open-ended problem.

00:00:25.790 --> 00:00:28.350
And almost every project is created with a team,

00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:30.500
which makes it so much more fun.

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:35.890
Our pedagogy is based on an architectural model

00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:38.840
that is really about a design process.

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:40.500
It's teaching critical thinking.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:44.700
It's teaching the ability to empower your own ideas

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:48.740
and go through an iterative creative process that is collaborative,

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:51.720
that it has a critical loop to it.

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:55.070
The first couple of days, we do a lot of introductory work

00:00:55.070 --> 00:00:58.014
where the students get to learn deeply about the problems

00:00:58.014 --> 00:00:58.930
that we're addressing.

00:00:58.930 --> 00:01:03.240
And then we do a lot of brainstorming and ideation.

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:06.050
After that phase, after the students divide up into groups,

00:01:06.050 --> 00:01:08.820
I’ll go around to different groups and help them

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:12.290
both by giving feedback about their designs and their ideas,

00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:15.010
but also actually helping them technically.

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:18.950
So when I was at regular school, there was no chance for me

00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:20.840
to be myself, really.

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:24.630
What's really great about NuVu is it's all what I want to do.

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:27.740
I'm very passionate about my projects I'm working on,

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:29.680
and I get very interested in them.

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:31.750
It's very open and independent.

00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:34.000
And while most people would think that's a bad thing--

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:36.208
kids could just do whatever they want-- you're really

00:01:36.208 --> 00:01:37.590
given a task here that's great.

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:40.810
And when you're into the task, you really want to just get it done.

00:01:40.810 --> 00:01:44.220
I came to NuVu because I like building things,

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:47.260
and I thought that NuVu would be a best fit for me for building my ideas

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:49.120
and making them come to life.

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:52.090
And I love regular school, but I thought it would be very cool.

00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:54.500
I'm really interested in the engineering and designing

00:01:54.500 --> 00:02:01.785
aspect of building, and prototyping, and stuff, and all this cool software work.

00:02:01.785 --> 00:02:04.930
So right now, I'm working on a hand drive wheelchair attachment.

00:02:04.930 --> 00:02:07.245
And so what it does is it uses a rationing mechanism,

00:02:07.245 --> 00:02:09.370
which means it has free resistance in one direction

00:02:09.370 --> 00:02:10.830
and locks in the other direction.

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:13.330
But what we've done is we've stuck two on top of each other,

00:02:13.330 --> 00:02:16.440
so when you switch the handle, it actually goes backwards.

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:19.590
And the super cool thing about this, which is like crazy to make,

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:22.430
was that this one actually has a gearing up ratio.

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:26.260
So it's faster than if you would just push the wheel 1 to 1.

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:29.990
So this is one of the projects that I recently created,

00:02:29.990 --> 00:02:33.640
which is a music box that attaches to a bus pole.

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:38.486
So the need for it is that when you sit at a bus stop, obviously,

00:02:38.486 --> 00:02:39.610
everyone's on their phones.

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:41.610
And in the winter, it's cold, and there's really nothing

00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:42.734
to do while waiting for it.

00:02:42.734 --> 00:02:47.650
So the purpose of this was to create an interactive piece of art that brings

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:49.950
people together in a communal way.

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:53.060
So this is a bus pole that everyone sees at the bus stop.

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:57.160
And what we created was a cylindrical music box that attaches to it.

00:02:57.160 --> 00:02:59.170
This is the music box part.

00:02:59.170 --> 00:03:01.910
There's a resonation system down here.

00:03:01.910 --> 00:03:04.450
So these are the actual tongs that are tuned.

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:07.950
[PLAYING  MUSICAL NOTES]

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:11.030
At any given point, we just want to track where

00:03:11.030 --> 00:03:12.700
the students are in their process.

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:15.880
We track their creative skills, and mostly for us, this

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:19.830
revolves around the critique process, how

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:24.490
the students are taking that critique and synthesizing it

00:03:24.490 --> 00:03:28.360
into design decisions, how they are communicating their ideas,

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:30.220
and how they are collaborating with others.

00:03:30.220 --> 00:03:33.680
I think the critique process is something that is definitely

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:36.120
an adjustment period for some kids.

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:39.270
And I think part of it is that when you're a creative person, when

00:03:39.270 --> 00:03:43.660
you have creative ideas, it's really hard to separate them from yourself.

00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:46.880
And so it's very common for students to get defensive,

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:51.440
or to just write off the critiques, just because it's so hard to really put

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:53.920
yourself out there.

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:56.890
And I've definitely had critiques that have gone really well,

00:03:56.890 --> 00:04:00.150
and I've also had critiques where my eyes water a little bit.

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:03.250
But also, I know that these people are here to help me.

00:04:03.250 --> 00:04:05.780
These people are my family at NuVu.

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:09.700
Our hope is that a student goes away from this experience

00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:13.610
with the idea that what they believe in and what they think matters,

00:04:13.610 --> 00:04:16.450
and that it's really about the process, not

00:04:16.450 --> 00:04:18.571
about the thing that comes out the other end.

00:04:18.571 --> 00:04:20.320
I think the most important piece is really

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:24.040
creating learning environments that engage the students.

00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:26.890
And you don't engage the students by giving them problems

00:04:26.890 --> 00:04:29.555
that you already know the answer for.

00:04:29.555 --> 00:04:31.920
You engage them by giving problems that they

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:34.100
are excited about, that they feel that they

00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:36.630
could change the world by solving them.

00:04:36.630 --> 00:04:39.980
[MUSIC PLAYING]

